    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm studying for sec+ and i'm kinda stuck on something. if we are already hashing passwords, why do we actually need to add a \"salt\"? wouldnt the hash already make it secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hashing passwords is great, but a salt adds vital protection against rainbow table attacks. Without a salt, identical passwords produce identical hashes, allowing attackers to pre-calculate hashes for common passwords. By adding a unique random string (the salt) to each password before hashing, the resulting hash is unique even for the same password, dramatically increasing security.",
